# Valgrind suppressions for PCRE2's JIT — applied ONLY to the CRS test binary.
#
# Kept out of valgrind.supp, and switched on per-binary by
# scripts/valgrind-runner.sh, because the single entry below is unavoidably
# broad: it can only match on unsymbolised frames, so it would also hide a real
# 16-byte invalid read in libmodsecurity or in this crate. Confining it to
# tests/crs.rs keeps every other test binary checked at full strength.
#
# What it hides, and why that is not a bug:
#
#   ==…== Invalid read of size 16
#   ==…==    at 0x8D28FBD: ???
#   ==…==    by 0xB461E7F: ???
#   ==…==  Address 0xb461e8f is 15 bytes inside a block of size 27 alloc'd
#   ==…==    at 0x4846FA3: operator new(unsigned long)
#   ==…==    by 0x51DB85A: std::__cxx11::basic_string<…>::_M_construct<char*>
#   ==…==    by 0x4974433: modsecurity::RuleWithActions::executeTransformations
#
# libmodsecurity links libpcre2-8, whose JIT-compiled matchers scan subjects
# with unaligned 16-byte vector loads. A load may start inside the subject
# string and run past its end — never across a page boundary, so it cannot
# fault, but Memcheck sees the tail bytes as unaddressable. Both frames are
# JIT-generated code with no symbols or unwind information, which is why the
# stack is two anonymous frames deep.
#
# This is a known PCRE2 property, not a defect in the subject string's owner:
# upstream's remedy is to build PCRE2 with --enable-valgrind, which teaches the
# JIT to mark those reads. The distribution's libpcre2-8 is not built that way,
# and nothing in this crate chooses whether the JIT is used — libmodsecurity
# enables it at build time. The errors appear only here because the CRS drives
# far more, and far more complex, regexes than the hand-written rules in the
# other tests do.
#
# --partial-loads-ok, which would cover exactly this case, does not help: it
# applies only to *naturally aligned* vector loads, and these are not aligned.
#
# If PCRE2 is ever rebuilt with Valgrind support, delete this file and the
# branch in scripts/valgrind-runner.sh that reaches for it.
{
   pcre2-jit-unaligned-subject-overread
   Memcheck:Addr16
   obj:*
   obj:*
}

# The same vector loads, one step later. Having read past the end of the
# subject, the JIT compares the whole 16-byte register against the pattern and
# discards the lanes beyond the string, so the branch itself is taken on bytes
# Memcheck has marked undefined. The stack is the same pair of anonymous
# JIT frames:
#
#   ==…== Conditional jump or move depends on uninitialised value(s)
#   ==…==    at 0x9136A76: ???
#   ==…==    by 0x8921B0F: ???
{
   pcre2-jit-branch-on-overread-tail
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*
   obj:*
}
